#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Add Java version evolution + Spring Boot deep-dive sections to block 1."""

with open('/home/user/IV/senior-architect-prep.html', 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    content = f.read()

# New sections to inject. These go into the `sections` array of the
# first DATA block ("Java Core & Spring Boot — Deep Dive").
# IMPORTANT: answers are inside backtick template literals — no backticks, no ${ } allowed.

new_sections = r''',
    {
      h: "Java version evolution — 8, 11, 17, 21, 24 (know what landed and why)",
      items: [
        {q: "Java 8 was the watershed release. Walk me through what it introduced and how it changed the way we write Java.", tag:"crit", a:`<p>Java 8 (March 2014) is the most important release in the language's history — it dragged Java into the functional era and is still the baseline many enterprises run. If you understand 8 deeply, most later features are refinements.</p><hr><strong>The headline features:</strong><ul><li><strong>Lambda expressions</strong> — anonymous functions as values. <code>(a, b) -> a + b</code>. Backed by <code>invokedynamic</code> bytecode (not anonymous inner classes), so no extra .class file per lambda and the JIT can optimise aggressively.</li><li><strong>Functional interfaces</strong> — any interface with a single abstract method (SAM). <code>@FunctionalInterface</code> enforces it. The <code>java.util.function</code> package gave us <code>Function</code>, <code>Supplier</code>, <code>Consumer</code>, <code>Predicate</code>, <code>BiFunction</code>, etc.</li><li><strong>Streams API</strong> — declarative, pipelined data processing. <code>list.stream().filter(...).map(...).collect(...)</code>. Lazy intermediate ops, one terminal op. Supports <code>.parallelStream()</code> backed by the common ForkJoinPool.</li><li><strong>Optional&lt;T&gt;</strong> — a container that may or may not hold a value, to make absence explicit and reduce NPEs. Meant for return types, not fields or parameters.</li><li><strong>Default and static methods on interfaces</strong> — interfaces can now carry implementation. This is how <code>Collection.stream()</code> was added without breaking every existing implementor. Enabled API evolution.</li><li><strong>java.time (JSR-310)</strong> — finally a sane date/time API. <code>LocalDate</code>, <code>LocalDateTime</code>, <code>ZonedDateTime</code>, <code>Instant</code>, <code>Duration</code>, <code>Period</code>. Immutable, thread-safe — the death of <code>SimpleDateFormat</code> concurrency bugs.</li><li><strong>CompletableFuture</strong> — composable async programming. <code>thenApply</code>, <code>thenCompose</code>, <code>thenCombine</code>, <code>exceptionally</code>. The foundation of non-blocking pipelines before reactive took over.</li><li><strong>Metaspace replaces PermGen</strong> — class metadata moved from a fixed-size heap region (PermGen, source of endless <code>OutOfMemoryError: PermGen space</code>) to native memory that grows dynamically. <code>-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize</code> caps it.</li><li><strong>Nashorn</strong> — a JS engine (later removed in 15). Mostly trivia now.</li></ul><hr><strong>The Streams gotchas a senior must know:</strong><ul><li>Streams are <strong>single-use</strong> — consume once, then it is closed. Reusing throws <code>IllegalStateException</code>.</li><li><strong>Parallel streams use the common ForkJoinPool</strong> — sized to CPU cores minus one. A blocking operation inside a parallel stream starves the whole JVM's parallel work. Never do I/O in a parallel stream without a custom pool.</li><li><code>Optional.get()</code> without <code>isPresent()</code> is just NPE with extra steps. Prefer <code>orElse</code>, <code>orElseGet</code> (lazy), <code>orElseThrow</code>, <code>map</code>.</li><li><code>orElse(expensiveCall())</code> always evaluates the argument — use <code>orElseGet(() -> expensiveCall())</code> to make it lazy.</li></ul><hr><span class="tradeoff">Architect framing:</span> "Java 8 is still the floor for a lot of enterprise estates, especially WebSphere shops. When I assess a codebase I look at whether it actually adopted the idioms — streams, Optional at boundaries, java.time everywhere — or whether it is Java 5 written on an 8 runtime. The latter is a modernisation smell."`},
        {q: "Java 9 through 11 — what changed, and why is Java 11 such a common enterprise baseline?", tag:"high", a:`<p>The 9-11 window is where Oracle switched to a six-month release cadence and introduced the LTS model. Java 11 (Sep 2018) became the first LTS after 8 and is hugely common in production.</p><hr><strong>Java 9 (Sep 2017):</strong><ul><li><strong>JPMS — the Java Platform Module System (Project Jigsaw)</strong>. <code>module-info.java</code>, <code>requires</code>, <code>exports</code>. Strong encapsulation of the JDK itself (<code>jdk.internal.*</code> now hidden). The most disruptive change — broke reflection-heavy libraries. Most apps still do NOT use modules, but the JDK is modularised, which is what enables <code>jlink</code>.</li><li><strong>jlink</strong> — build a custom minimal runtime image with only the modules you need. Smaller container images.</li><li><strong>Collection factory methods</strong> — <code>List.of()</code>, <code>Set.of()</code>, <code>Map.of()</code> — immutable, concise.</li><li><strong>Private interface methods</strong>, try-with-resources on effectively-final vars, <code>Stream.takeWhile/dropWhile/iterate</code>.</li><li><strong>G1 becomes the default GC.</strong></li></ul><hr><strong>Java 10 (Mar 2018):</strong><ul><li><strong>var — local-variable type inference.</strong> <code>var list = new ArrayList&lt;String&gt;();</code>. Only for locals; the type is still static, just inferred. Improves readability when the RHS is obvious; hurts it when it is not.</li><li>Application Class-Data Sharing, parallel full GC for G1.</li></ul><hr><strong>Java 11 (Sep 2018) — LTS:</strong><ul><li><strong>Standardised HttpClient</strong> (<code>java.net.http</code>) — HTTP/2, WebSocket, sync and async (<code>CompletableFuture</code>). Replaces the ancient <code>HttpURLConnection</code> and removes the need for Apache HttpClient in many cases.</li><li><strong>Run a single source file directly</strong> — <code>java Hello.java</code>, no compile step. Great for scripts.</li><li><code>String</code> additions — <code>isBlank</code>, <code>strip</code>, <code>lines</code>, <code>repeat</code>.</li><li><code>var</code> usable in lambda parameters (for annotations).</li><li><strong>Flight Recorder + Mission Control open-sourced</strong>; <strong>ZGC and Epsilon</strong> arrive (experimental).</li><li><strong>Removed</strong> Java EE and CORBA modules (JAXB, JAX-WS) — a migration pain point from 8.</li></ul><hr><strong>Why 11 is a baseline:</strong> first free LTS after 8 (post the Oracle licensing change), long vendor support (Adoptium/Temurin, Amazon Corretto, Azul), and the last comfortable stop before the bigger language changes of 17. Spring Boot 2.x runs happily on it.<hr><span class="gotcha">Migration gotcha 8 to 11:</span> removed JAXB/JAX-WS means you must add them as explicit dependencies. Reflection into JDK internals (common in old Hibernate, Mockito, bytecode libs) triggers <code>InaccessibleObjectException</code> — you either upgrade those libs or add <code>--add-opens</code> flags. Most 8 to 11 migrations are blocked by a transitive dependency, not your own code.`},
        {q: "Java 17 — records, sealed classes, pattern matching, text blocks. Explain each and when you'd reach for it.", tag:"crit", a:`<p>Java 17 (Sep 2021) is the LTS that Spring Boot 3 made mandatory. It is where modern Java really starts to feel different from 8. These features compose into a much more expressive, data-oriented style.</p><hr><strong>Records</strong> (finalised in 16)<ul><li>Transparent, immutable data carriers. <code>public record Point(int x, int y) {}</code> auto-generates the constructor, accessors (<code>x()</code>, <code>y()</code>), <code>equals</code>, <code>hashCode</code>, <code>toString</code>.</li><li><strong>Compact canonical constructor</strong> for validation: <code>public record Range(int lo, int hi){ public Range{ if(lo&gt;hi) throw new IllegalArgumentException(); } }</code>.</li><li>Perfect for DTOs, value objects, query results, events. Replaces a huge amount of Lombok <code>@Value</code> boilerplate with a language feature.</li><li>Can implement interfaces, cannot extend classes (implicitly final, extend <code>Record</code>).</li></ul><hr><strong>Sealed classes/interfaces</strong> (finalised in 17)<ul><li><code>public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square, Triangle {}</code> — restricts which types can implement/extend.</li><li>Makes a type hierarchy <strong>closed and known to the compiler</strong>, which powers exhaustive pattern matching (no <code>default</code> needed if you cover all permitted types).</li><li>Models algebraic data types — "a Shape is exactly one of these three". Domain modelling becomes precise.</li></ul><hr><strong>Pattern matching for instanceof</strong> (finalised in 16)<ul><li><code>if (obj instanceof String s) { use s; }</code> — test and bind in one step, no explicit cast. Eliminates the classic test-cast-assign triple.</li></ul><hr><strong>Text blocks</strong> (finalised in 15)<ul><li>Multi-line string literals with triple quotes. Great for JSON, SQL, HTML in tests and config without escaping hell.<pre><code>String sql = """
    SELECT id, name
    FROM users
    WHERE active = true
    """;</code></pre></li></ul><hr><strong>Switch expressions</strong> (finalised in 14)<ul><li>Switch that returns a value, arrow syntax, no fall-through, exhaustiveness checking. <code>var label = switch(day){ case MON, TUE -&gt; "early"; default -&gt; "later"; };</code></li></ul><hr><strong>Other 17 notables:</strong> strong encapsulation of JDK internals is now enforced (the <code>--illegal-access</code> escape hatch is gone), new <code>RandomGenerator</code> interface, always-strict floating point.<hr><span class="tradeoff">Architect framing:</span> "Records + sealed types + pattern matching together let you model a domain as data and exhaustively handle each case with the compiler enforcing completeness. When a new variant is added, every switch that is not exhaustive fails to compile — that is a refactoring safety net you do not get in Java 8. I push teams to adopt this for domain events and command handling."`},
        {q: "Java 21 — virtual threads and structured concurrency. This is the big one for backend services. Explain it properly.", tag:"crit", a:`<p>Java 21 (Sep 2023) is the current major LTS and the most consequential release for server-side Java since 8. Virtual threads (Project Loom) change the fundamental concurrency model for I/O-bound services.</p><hr><strong>The problem virtual threads solve:</strong><ul><li>Platform threads map 1:1 to OS threads. They are expensive (~1MB stack, kernel-scheduled), so you pool them. A typical service caps at a few hundred to a few thousand.</li><li>For I/O-bound work (most enterprise services — call a DB, call another service, wait), threads spend their life <strong>blocked, doing nothing but holding memory.</strong> Throughput is capped by pool size, not by CPU.</li><li>The reactive workaround (WebFlux, RxJava) fixed throughput but at a brutal cost: callback-coloured code, no usable stack traces, debugging hell, a steep learning curve.</li></ul><hr><strong>What virtual threads do:</strong><ul><li>Lightweight threads managed by the <strong>JVM, not the OS.</strong> Thousands-to-millions are cheap (a few hundred bytes, grow on demand).</li><li>When a virtual thread hits a blocking call, the JVM <strong>unmounts</strong> it from its carrier (platform) thread and parks it, freeing the carrier to run another virtual thread. When the I/O completes, it remounts. The blocking is virtual; the carrier never actually blocks.</li><li><strong>You write plain blocking, synchronous code</strong> — the simple style — and get the scalability of async. Stack traces work. Debuggers work. <code>ThreadLocal</code> works.</li><li>Create with <code>Thread.ofVirtual().start(r)</code> or <code>Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()</code> — one virtual thread per task, no pooling.</li><li>Spring Boot 3.2+: <code>spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true</code> and Tomcat serves each request on a virtual thread.</li></ul><hr><strong>The pinning gotcha (critical):</strong><ul><li>A virtual thread that blocks inside a <code>synchronized</code> block <strong>pins</strong> to its carrier — it cannot unmount, so you lose the benefit and can deadlock the carrier pool under load.</li><li>Fix: replace <code>synchronized</code> with <code>ReentrantLock</code> around blocking calls. (Java 24 largely removes this pinning limitation — see below.)</li><li>Also pins on native/JNI calls.</li></ul><hr><strong>Structured concurrency</strong> (preview in 21)<ul><li><code>StructuredTaskScope</code> — treat a group of concurrent subtasks as a single unit. Fork subtasks, join them, and if one fails or the deadline hits, the rest are cancelled. No leaked threads, clear error propagation, parent-child relationship visible in thread dumps.</li></ul><hr><strong>Other 21 features:</strong> record patterns (destructure records in switch/instanceof), pattern matching for switch finalised (including <code>null</code> and guarded patterns), sequenced collections (<code>getFirst</code>/<code>getLast</code>/<code>reversed</code>), generational ZGC.<hr><span class="tradeoff">Architect decision:</span> "For a new I/O-bound service in 2026 I default to Java 21 + virtual threads + plain Spring MVC rather than WebFlux. You get reactive-level throughput with imperative-level simplicity. I only reach for WebFlux when I genuinely need backpressure or streaming semantics. The one rule I enforce: audit for <code>synchronized</code> around blocking I/O and migrate to <code>ReentrantLock</code>, and watch for pinning with <code>-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full</code>."`},
        {q: "Java 21 virtual threads vs reactive (WebFlux) — when does each still make sense?", tag:"high", a:`<p>This is a sharp architect question because the easy answer ("just use virtual threads now") is incomplete. They solve overlapping but not identical problems.</p><hr><strong>Virtual threads give you:</strong><ul><li>High concurrency for blocking I/O with the <strong>simple imperative model</strong> — readable code, real stack traces, ordinary debugging.</li><li>Drop-in for existing blocking codebases — JDBC, JPA, RestTemplate all just work and scale.</li><li>One thread per request; no thread-pool tuning for I/O.</li></ul><strong>What they do NOT give you:</strong><ul><li><strong>Backpressure.</strong> A virtual thread per request means a flood of requests creates a flood of virtual threads, all hitting your DB. You still need a connection pool / semaphore / bulkhead as the real limiter. Virtual threads remove the thread bottleneck but not downstream capacity limits.</li><li>Streaming/dataflow composition.</li></ul><hr><strong>Reactive (WebFlux/Reactor) gives you:</strong><ul><li><strong>Backpressure as a first-class concept</strong> — the consumer signals how much it can take; the producer respects it. Essential for streaming, event pipelines, slow consumers.</li><li>Rich operators for composing async dataflows (<code>flatMap</code>, <code>buffer</code>, <code>window</code>, <code>retryWhen</code>, <code>zip</code>).</li><li>Non-blocking all the way down IF your drivers are reactive (R2DBC, reactive Mongo, reactive Kafka).</li></ul><strong>Its costs:</strong> coloured functions (everything returns <code>Mono</code>/<code>Flux</code>), painful debugging, hard onboarding, and it only pays off if the <em>entire</em> chain is non-blocking — one blocking JDBC call poisons the event loop.</li></ul><hr><strong>The decision:</strong><table><tr><th>Need</th><th>Choose</th></tr><tr><td>CRUD/REST service, blocking DB, high concurrency</td><td>Virtual threads + MVC</td></tr><tr><td>Streaming, SSE, websockets, slow consumers</td><td>Reactive</td></tr><tr><td>Need backpressure across the pipeline</td><td>Reactive</td></tr><tr><td>Legacy blocking code you want to scale cheaply</td><td>Virtual threads</td></tr><tr><td>Team unfamiliar with reactive, time-pressured</td><td>Virtual threads</td></tr></table><hr><span class="tradeoff">My stance:</span> "Virtual threads make reactive unnecessary for the majority of request/response services — which is most of what we build. I keep reactive in the toolbox specifically for streaming and backpressure-sensitive workloads. Mixing: you can even run Reactor on top of virtual threads for the composition operators without the non-blocking-driver requirement, though that is a niche."`},
        {q: "Java 22, 23, 24 and the LTS cadence — what should I know about the latest, and how do you reason about which version to run?", tag:"med", a:`<p>You will not be quizzed line-by-line on non-LTS releases, but a senior should know the cadence, the direction of travel, and how to choose a version for a real project.</p><hr><strong>The release model:</strong><ul><li><strong>Six-month feature releases</strong> (March and September). Each is production-grade but supported only until the next one.</li><li><strong>LTS every two years</strong> — 8, 11, 17, 21, and <strong>25 (Sep 2025)</strong> is the next LTS. LTS gets years of updates from vendors (Temurin, Corretto, Azul, Red Hat).</li><li><strong>Preview features</strong> ship behind <code>--enable-preview</code> to gather feedback before finalisation — try them, do not run them in prod.</li></ul><hr><strong>Recent highlights (22-24, 2024-2025):</strong><ul><li><strong>Stream Gatherers</strong> (finalised ~24) — a standard way to write custom intermediate stream operations (<code>Stream.gather(...)</code>), the long-missing counterpart to custom Collectors. Windowing, folding, custom stateful transforms.</li><li><strong>Virtual thread pinning largely fixed (24)</strong> — <code>synchronized</code> blocks no longer pin virtual threads in most cases, removing the biggest Loom footgun.</li><li><strong>Ahead-of-Time class loading and linking (Project Leyden, 24)</strong> — faster JVM startup and warmup by caching class-loading work; meaningful for serverless and short-lived JVMs.</li><li><strong>Foreign Function and Memory API</strong> finalised (21/22) — the modern, safe replacement for JNI to call native code and manage off-heap memory.</li><li><strong>Structured concurrency and scoped values</strong> continue maturing toward finalisation.</li></ul><hr><strong>How to choose a version:</strong><ol><li><strong>Default to the latest LTS</strong> (21 today, 25 going forward) for anything with a multi-year lifespan. Vendor support and stability matter more than newest syntax.</li><li>Match your framework floor: <strong>Spring Boot 3.x requires 17+</strong>; to use virtual threads cleanly you want 21.</li><li>Use a non-LTS only if you need a specific just-landed feature and you are comfortable upgrading every six months.</li><li>Pick a <strong>vendor and a build</strong> deliberately — Eclipse Temurin is the common neutral default; Corretto if you are AWS-heavy; OpenJ9/Semeru in IBM/WebSphere shops for its lower memory footprint.</li></ol><hr><span class="tradeoff">Architect framing:</span> "I run the latest LTS in production and keep a CI job building against the newest feature release to catch breakage early. That way the next LTS jump (21 to 25) is a non-event instead of a six-month project. Staying two LTS versions behind — still on 11 or 8 — is a real risk: you miss virtual threads, you accumulate a harder migration, and you fall off the security-update cliff."`}
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Spring Boot — deep internals (auto-config, bean lifecycle, Boot 3 / native)",
      items: [
        {q: "Explain Spring Boot auto-configuration end to end. How does @SpringBootApplication actually wire up a working app from almost no code?", tag:"crit", a:`<p>Auto-configuration is the heart of Spring Boot's "convention over configuration" magic. Interviewers love this because a surface answer ("it just configures things") is obviously shallow; the real mechanism shows you understand the framework.</p><hr><strong>The trigger chain:</strong><ol><li><code>@SpringBootApplication</code> is a meta-annotation combining three things:<ul><li><code>@SpringBootConfiguration</code> (a specialised <code>@Configuration</code>),</li><li><code>@ComponentScan</code> (scan this package and below for your beans),</li><li><code>@EnableAutoConfiguration</code> — the magic switch.</li></ul></li><li><code>@EnableAutoConfiguration</code> imports <code>AutoConfigurationImportSelector</code>.</li><li>That selector reads candidate configuration classes from <strong><code>META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports</code></strong> (Boot 2.7+; older Boot used the <code>spring.factories</code> key <code>EnableAutoConfiguration</code>). Every starter on the classpath contributes entries.</li><li>Each candidate is a <code>@Configuration</code> class guarded by <strong><code>@Conditional</code></strong> annotations. Spring evaluates the conditions and only applies the ones that match.</li></ol><hr><strong>The conditional annotations that drive it:</strong><ul><li><code>@ConditionalOnClass</code> — apply only if a class is on the classpath (e.g. configure JPA only if Hibernate is present).</li><li><code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code> — apply only if the user has NOT defined their own bean of this type. <strong>This is why your custom bean always wins</strong> — auto-config backs off.</li><li><code>@ConditionalOnProperty</code> — apply only if a property has a given value.</li><li><code>@ConditionalOnBean</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnWebApplication</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnResource</code>, etc.</li></ul><hr><strong>Ordering and back-off:</strong> user configuration is processed so that user beans are registered, then auto-config runs with <code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code> checks — hence user beans override defaults. <code>@AutoConfigureBefore</code>/<code>@AutoConfigureAfter</code> order auto-config classes relative to each other.<hr><strong>How to see what happened:</strong><ul><li>Run with <code>--debug</code> (or <code>debug=true</code>) to print the <strong>Conditions Evaluation Report</strong>: which auto-configs matched (Positive matches) and which were skipped and why (Negative matches). This is the single best tool for "why is this bean / not configured?"</li><li>Actuator <code>/conditions</code> endpoint exposes the same at runtime.</li></ul><hr><span class="gotcha">Common real-world issue:</span> "My DataSource is not what I expect." Almost always two starters both provide a candidate and one wins by classpath order, or a property is missing so <code>@ConditionalOnProperty</code> skips it. The conditions report tells you instantly. <hr><span class="tradeoff">Architect framing:</span> "Auto-config is just conditional <code>@Bean</code> definitions contributed by jars on the classpath. Once you internalise that, Boot stops being magic. When I write a shared internal starter, I follow the same pattern: a <code>@Configuration</code> with <code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code> so teams can override, registered via the <code>AutoConfiguration.imports</code> file."`},
        {q: "Walk me through the Spring bean lifecycle — from definition to destruction — including BeanPostProcessor and where AOP proxies get created.", tag:"crit", a:`<p>Understanding the bean lifecycle explains a huge amount of "why doesn't my @Transactional / @Async / @Cacheable work?" The answer is almost always about proxying, which happens at a specific lifecycle phase.</p><hr><strong>The phases, in order:</strong><ol><li><strong>Bean definition loading</strong> — <code>@ComponentScan</code>, <code>@Bean</code> methods, and imported configs produce <code>BeanDefinition</code> objects in the <code>BeanFactory</code>. Nothing instantiated yet.</li><li><strong>BeanFactoryPostProcessors run</strong> — can modify bean definitions before instantiation. <code>PropertySourcesPlaceholderConfigurer</code> (resolves <code>$&#123;...&#125;</code>) and <code>ConfigurationClassPostProcessor</code> work here.</li><li><strong>Instantiation</strong> — constructor called (constructor injection happens here).</li><li><strong>Populate properties</strong> — field/setter dependency injection.</li><li><strong>Aware callbacks</strong> — <code>BeanNameAware</code>, <code>ApplicationContextAware</code>, etc.</li><li><strong>BeanPostProcessor.postProcessBeforeInitialization</strong> — runs for every bean. This is where <code>@Autowired</code>/<code>@Value</code> resolution (via <code>AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor</code>) and <code>@PostConstruct</code> wiring is prepared.</li><li><strong>Initialization callbacks</strong> — <code>@PostConstruct</code>, then <code>InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet()</code>, then a custom <code>initMethod</code>.</li><li><strong>BeanPostProcessor.postProcessAfterInitialization</strong> — <strong>this is where AOP proxies are created.</strong> <code>AbstractAutoProxyCreator</code> wraps the raw bean in a proxy if any advisors (transactional, async, cacheable, custom aspects) apply. The bean handed to the rest of the context is the PROXY, not the original.</li><li><strong>Bean is in service</strong> (singletons cached in the context).</li><li><strong>Destruction</strong> — on context close: <code>@PreDestroy</code>, then <code>DisposableBean.destroy()</code>, then custom <code>destroyMethod</code>.</li></ol><hr><strong>Why this matters — the self-invocation trap:</strong> because behaviour like <code>@Transactional</code> lives in the <strong>proxy</strong> created in step 8, calling an annotated method from <em>within the same bean</em> (<code>this.doTx()</code>) bypasses the proxy entirely — the annotation is silently ignored. The call must come through the proxy reference (inject self, or split into another bean) for the advice to fire. Same for <code>@Async</code>, <code>@Cacheable</code>, <code>@Retryable</code>.<hr><strong>Proxy mechanics:</strong> Spring uses <strong>JDK dynamic proxies</strong> if the bean implements an interface, otherwise <strong>CGLIB</strong> subclass proxies (Boot defaults to CGLIB via <code>proxyTargetClass=true</code>). CGLIB cannot proxy <code>final</code> classes/methods — another silent-failure source.<hr><span class="gotcha">Classic bug:</span> <code>@Transactional</code> on a <code>private</code> method — AOP can only advise externally-visible, proxied calls, so it does nothing. Must be <code>public</code> and called through the proxy.<hr><span class="tradeoff">Architect framing:</span> "Ninety percent of 'my annotation does not work' tickets are the proxy boundary — self-invocation, private methods, or final classes. I teach teams the rule: annotated cross-cutting behaviour only fires on calls that cross the proxy. Once they hold that mental model the whole class of bugs disappears."`},
        {q: "What actually is a Spring Boot starter, and how would you build your own internal starter for shared company concerns?", tag:"high", a:`<p>Starters are how Boot packages opinionated, ready-to-go capabilities. Building an internal one is a common platform-team task and a strong signal of architect-level Spring knowledge.</p><hr><strong>What a starter IS:</strong><ul><li>A starter is essentially <strong>a dependency aggregator plus auto-configuration.</strong> <code>spring-boot-starter-web</code> contains almost no code — it is a POM that pulls in Spring MVC, Jackson, validation, and embedded Tomcat at compatible versions.</li><li>The actual wiring lives in <strong><code>spring-boot-autoconfigure</code></strong>, whose conditional <code>@Configuration</code> classes activate when the relevant classes appear on the classpath (because the starter put them there).</li><li>So "add one dependency, get a working feature" = curated transitive deps + conditional auto-config that detects them.</li></ul><hr><strong>Building your own (e.g. a company "observability + security defaults" starter):</strong><ol><li>Create an <strong>autoconfigure module</strong> with the actual <code>@AutoConfiguration</code> classes:<pre><code>@AutoConfiguration
@ConditionalOnClass(MeterRegistry.class)
@ConditionalOnProperty(prefix="acme.metrics", name="enabled",
                       matchIfMissing=true)
public class AcmeMetricsAutoConfiguration {
  @Bean
  @ConditionalOnMissingBean
  AcmeTagger acmeTagger(AcmeProps p){ return new AcmeTagger(p); }
}</code></pre></li><li>Register it in <code>META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports</code> (one fully-qualified class name per line).</li><li>Expose tunables via a <code>@ConfigurationProperties("acme.metrics")</code> class with sane defaults and Bean Validation.</li><li>Create a thin <strong>starter module</strong> (a POM) that depends on the autoconfigure module plus the required libraries, so teams add ONE dependency.</li><li>Follow the naming convention: third-party starters are <code>acme-spring-boot-starter</code> (the <code>spring-boot-starter-*</code> prefix is reserved for the Spring team).</li><li>Always guard beans with <code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code> so consuming apps can override your defaults.</li></ol><hr><strong>Why a platform team does this:</strong> consistent logging format, tracing propagation, security headers, actuator config, and metric tags across dozens of services — defined once, adopted by adding a dependency, overridable where needed. It is how you enforce standards without copy-paste.<hr><span class="tradeoff">Architect framing:</span> "Internal starters are the highest-leverage Spring pattern for a platform team. The discipline is: opinionated defaults, but always <code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code> and a properties class so a team with a good reason can deviate. A starter that cannot be overridden becomes a thing teams fork or fight."`},
        {q: "Spring Boot 3 and Spring Framework 6 — what changed, and what does GraalVM native image / AOT mean for how we build services?", tag:"crit", a:`<p>Spring Boot 3.0 (Nov 2022) was a major-version reset. Knowing what it forced and what it unlocked is very current and architect-relevant.</p><hr><strong>The breaking baseline changes:</strong><ul><li><strong>Java 17 minimum.</strong> Boot 3 will not run on 8 or 11 — a hard floor that gates migration.</li><li><strong>Jakarta EE namespace migration</strong> — every <code>javax.*</code> import became <code>jakarta.*</code> (<code>javax.persistence</code> to <code>jakarta.persistence</code>, <code>javax.servlet</code> to <code>jakarta.servlet</code>). This is the single biggest mechanical migration cost and it ripples through every dependency that touches servlets/JPA/validation. Libraries must publish Jakarta-compatible versions.</li><li>Upgraded baselines for Hibernate 6, Tomcat 10, etc.</li></ul><hr><strong>The headline new capabilities:</strong><ul><li><strong>GraalVM native image support (AOT).</strong> Compile a Spring app ahead-of-time into a standalone native executable: <strong>startup in tens of milliseconds, drastically lower memory, no JVM warmup.</strong> Huge for serverless and scale-to-zero. Cost: a slower build, no runtime reflection/dynamic-proxy unless registered (RuntimeHints), and you lose JIT peak throughput for long-running workloads.</li><li><strong>Spring AOT engine</strong> — at build time, Spring pre-computes the bean definitions and proxies that it would normally derive via reflection at startup, emitting source/hints. This is what makes native image feasible and also speeds JVM startup.</li><li><strong>Observability overhaul</strong> — Micrometer <strong>Observation API</strong> unifies metrics and tracing: one <code>Observation</code> produces both a timer and a span. Micrometer Tracing replaces Spring Cloud Sleuth. First-class OpenTelemetry support.</li><li><strong>ProblemDetail (RFC 7807/9457)</strong> — standardised machine-readable error responses built in.</li><li><strong>Declarative HTTP clients</strong> via <code>@HttpExchange</code> interfaces backed by <code>WebClient</code>/<code>RestClient</code>; the new synchronous <strong>RestClient</strong> (Boot 3.2) gives a fluent, modern replacement for <code>RestTemplate</code>.</li><li><strong>Virtual thread support</strong> (3.2) — <code>spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true</code>.</li></ul><hr><strong>When native image is worth it:</strong><table><tr><th>Workload</th><th>Native?</th></tr><tr><td>Serverless / FaaS, scale-to-zero, frequent cold starts</td><td>Yes — startup + memory win is decisive</td></tr><tr><td>CLI tools, short-lived jobs</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Long-running high-throughput service</td><td>Usually no — JIT peak throughput beats AOT; pay warmup once</td></tr><tr><td>Heavy reflection/dynamic libraries</td><td>Careful — needs hints; can be painful</td></tr></table><hr><span class="tradeoff">Architect framing:</span> "Boot 3 has two stories. The boring-but-essential one is the Jakarta migration — mostly mechanical, but every dependency must be Jakarta-ready, so the long pole is your transitive deps, not your code. The exciting one is AOT/native: I reach for it on serverless and cold-start-sensitive workloads where 50ms startup and 80MB RSS change the economics, and I stay on the JVM for long-running services where JIT throughput wins. Either way, the Observation API migration is worth doing for unified metrics+tracing."`},
        {q: "Spring profiles and configuration property resolution — explain the precedence order and how you structure config across environments.", tag:"high", a:`<p>Configuration management is where a lot of production incidents are born (wrong value in prod, secret in the wrong place). A senior should know the precedence rules cold and have an opinion on structure.</p><hr><strong>Profiles:</strong><ul><li>A profile is a named logical group of config/beans — <code>dev</code>, <code>staging</code>, <code>prod</code>. Activate with <code>spring.profiles.active</code> (property, env var <code>SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE</code>, or CLI).</li><li><strong>Profile-specific files</strong> — <code>application-prod.yml</code> is loaded and overlaid on top of the base <code>application.yml</code> when <code>prod</code> is active.</li><li><code>@Profile("prod")</code> on a bean/config makes it active only under that profile (e.g. a real mail sender in prod, a no-op in dev).</li><li><strong>Profile groups</strong> (Boot 2.4+) — <code>spring.profiles.group.prod=prod,monitoring,db-prod</code> activates several at once.</li></ul><hr><strong>Property source precedence (highest wins, simplified):</strong><ol><li>Command-line arguments (<code>--server.port=9000</code>)</li><li>SPRING_APPLICATION_JSON / inline JSON</li><li>OS environment variables (<code>SERVER_PORT</code> — relaxed binding maps it to <code>server.port</code>)</li><li>Java system properties (<code>-Dserver.port</code>)</li><li><code>application-&#123;profile&#125;.yml</code> outside the jar (e.g. mounted ConfigMap)</li><li><code>application.yml</code> outside the jar</li><li><code>application-&#123;profile&#125;.yml</code> packaged in the jar</li><li><code>application.yml</code> packaged in the jar</li><li><code>@PropertySource</code> on <code>@Configuration</code></li><li>Default properties</li></ol><strong>The key idea:</strong> externalised config (env vars, mounted files) overrides packaged defaults, and profile-specific overrides base. This is exactly what you want for 12-factor deployments — same image, different env.<hr><strong>How I structure it (the architect's policy):</strong><ul><li><strong>In the jar:</strong> <code>application.yml</code> with defaults that are safe everywhere, plus thin <code>application-&#123;profile&#125;.yml</code> for non-secret, env-shaped differences (pool sizes, feature flags, log levels).</li><li><strong>Env vars / ConfigMap:</strong> per-environment non-secret values (URLs, hostnames).</li><li><strong>Secrets manager (Vault, AWS/Azure):</strong> credentials, keys — never in the image, never in git, audited on access. Spring Cloud Vault / Config can source these.</li><li><strong>Validate everything at startup</strong> with <code>@ConfigurationProperties</code> + Bean Validation so a missing/mistyped value fails fast with a clear message instead of an NPE at 2am.</li><li><strong>Test in CI</strong> that production-shaped config actually binds and validates.</li></ul><hr><span class="gotcha">Relaxed binding gotcha:</span> <code>server.port</code>, <code>SERVER_PORT</code>, <code>server_port</code> all bind to the same property — convenient, but it means an unexpected env var can silently override your config. When a value is "mysteriously wrong", check the environment first; env vars beat your YAML.<hr><span class="tradeoff">Architect framing:</span> "Config that is the same everywhere goes in the jar; config that varies but is not secret goes in the environment; secrets go in a vault. Profiles express the shape of an environment, not a place to hide credentials. And the non-negotiable: <code>@ConfigurationProperties</code> with validation so misconfiguration fails at startup, loudly."`}
      ]
    }'''

